## What was wrong
In `astropy/io/ascii/qdp.py`, the function `_line_type()` classifies each QDP file line with a regex built from `_command_re = r"READ [TS]ERR(\s+[0-9]+)+"` and compiled case-sensitively. A lower-case command line like `read serr 1 2` therefore matched nothing and raised `ValueError: Unrecognized QDP line: read serr 1 2`, even though QDP itself is case-insensitive. (The downstream command parsing already lower-cases keys — `command[1].lower()` — so the regex was the only case-sensitive spot.)

## The fix (minimal, source only, uncommitted)
One line in `astropy/io/ascii/qdp.py` (line 71):

```diff
-    _line_type_re = re.compile(_type_re)
+    _line_type_re = re.compile(_type_re, flags=re.IGNORECASE)
```

No tests touched, nothing committed — `git diff` shows exactly this one-line change in the working tree.

## Proof
- **Before fix** (pristine HEAD, stashed/reapplied): reading a file with `read serr 1 2` → `ValueError: Unrecognized QDP line: read serr 1 2` — the reported bug, reproduced.
- **After fix**: `_line_type("read serr 1 2")` → `'command'`; lower-case and upper-case versions of the same file parse to identical tables (`['col1', 'col1_err', 'col2', 'col2_err']`).
- **Regression check**: `pytest astropy/io/ascii/tests/test_qdp.py` → **8 passed**.
- Both findings recorded and proven (`FINDING-ff7c3b`, `FINDING-c43389`).

One environment note: this checkout had no built C extensions, so `import astropy` failed until I ran `python3 setup.py build_ext --inplace` (the same setup the sibling harness work dirs use). Build artifacts are gitignored, so the working tree still contains only the one-line fix. The `.venv` in `stack/campaign` has no pytest/astropy, so tests ran with system Python 3.12, which has the full dependency set.
